= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Put $s=z-z_0$. In the source's <complex beam parameter> convention,
$$
\frac1q=\frac{s+iz_R}{s^2+z_R^2}
=\frac1{R(z)}+i\frac{2}{kw(z)^2}.
$$
Substitution separates the transverse <wave amplitude> and <phase>:
$$
U=\frac{U_0e^{i(kz-\omega t)}}{s-iz_R}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{\rho^2}{w(z)^2}\right]
\exp\!\left[i\frac{k\rho^2}{2R(z)}\right].
$$
Thus the <Gaussian beam> is circularly symmetric, has a <Gaussian function> transverse <light intensity>, and narrows to a <beam waist> at $z_0$ before spreading by <diffraction>. Its <wavefront> is planar at the <beam waist>, converging before it and diverging after it. The quadratic transverse <phase> identifies the signed <radius of curvature>:
$$
\boxed{R(z)=s+\frac{z_R^2}{s},\qquad R(z_0)=\infty.}
$$
Define spot size as the <wave amplitude> $1/e$ <radius>, equivalently the <light intensity> $1/e^2$ <radius>. Then
$$
\boxed{w(z)^2=\frac{2(s^2+z_R^2)}{kz_R}
=w_0^2\left(1+\frac{s^2}{z_R^2}\right),\qquad
w_0^2=\frac{2z_R}{k}=\frac{\lambda z_R}{\pi}.}
$$
The <Rayleigh range> is where $w=\sqrt2w_0$; the far-field <beam divergence> half-angle is $\lambda/(\pi w_0)$. The factor $1/q$ also produces a longitudinal <phase> shift relative to a <plane wave>, changing by $\pi$ across the <beam waist>; its sign here follows the given $e^{i(kz-\omega t)}$ convention. The on-axis <light intensity> decreases as $1/(s^2+z_R^2)$ while transverse integration leaves the <power> constant. These are scalar <paraxial approximation> results.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

A <thin lens> has <ray transfer matrix>
$$
M=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-1/f&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The <complex beam parameter> transformation gives $q_2=q_1/(1-q_1/f)$, hence
$$
\boxed{\frac1{q_1}-\frac1{q_2}=\frac1f.}
$$
The sign is also obtained by multiplying the incident <wave amplitude> by the <thin lens> <phase> $\exp[-ik\rho^2/(2f)]$.

Choose the <thin lens> plane as $z=0$ and put $a=\pi w_0^2/\lambda$, the incident <Rayleigh range>. Since the incident <beam waist> lies there, $q_1=-ia$. Immediately after the <thin lens>,
$$
q_2=\frac{-ia}{1+ia/f}
=-\frac{fa^2}{f^2+a^2}-i\frac{f^2a}{f^2+a^2}.
$$
Subsequent free propagation adds $z$, so write $q(z)=z-z_f-iz_R'$. Comparing real and imaginary parts gives
$$
\boxed{z_f=\frac{fa^2}{f^2+a^2},\qquad
z_R'=\frac{f^2a}{f^2+a^2},\qquad
w_f^2=\frac{\lambda z_R'}\pi.}
$$
For a positive <focal length>, the new <beam waist> lies downstream but closer than the geometrical focus. At fixed $f,\lambda$, as $w_0\to\infty$,
$$
\boxed{z_f\to f,\quad z_R'\sim\frac{f^2\lambda}{\pi w_0^2}\to0,
\quad w_f\sim\frac{f\lambda}{\pi w_0}.}
$$
This is the formal unbounded-<aperture> scalar limit; eventually finite <aperture> and the <paraxial approximation> limit the extrapolation.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

A <chirp> is variation of the instantaneous <frequency> through a signal. Here the <group delay> relative to the pulse centre at the <optical lens> is
$$
t_{\rm in}(\omega)=\frac{d\psi}{d\omega}=2\eta(\omega-\omega_0).
$$
Consequently $\eta>0$ orders lower <frequencies> before higher <frequencies>; $\eta<0$ reverses that ordering. A representative strongly chirped <optical pulse> is
$$
E(t)=A(t)\cos\!\left(\omega_0t+\frac{t^2}{4\eta}+\phi_0\right),
\qquad \omega_{\rm inst}(t)=\omega_0+\frac{t}{2\eta},
$$
with a smooth localized envelope $A(t)$. The carrier oscillations become closer together towards later times for positive $\eta$ and farther apart for negative $\eta$.

The relation between the <spectral phase> and a locally assigned instantaneous <frequency> is a <stationary phase method>, not an exact reconstruction from <spectral phase> alone: the spectral <wave amplitude> also matters. For example, if the spectral envelope is $e^{-(\omega-\omega_0)^2/(2\sigma^2)}$, a <Fourier transform of a Gaussian> gives the exact real-field <phase> $\omega_0t+\eta t^2/(4|a|^2)$ up to a constant, where $a=1/(2\sigma^2)-i\eta$. The slope retains the sign of $\eta$ and approaches $1/(2\eta)$ for $|\eta|\sigma^2\gg1$.

\Image[2021-chirp-and-flying-focus.png]
{title=Opposite <chirps> and the velocity branches of a <flying focus>}

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

Place the <optical lens> at $z=0$. For a sufficiently broad incident <Gaussian beam>, the <beam waist> positions approach the <focal lengths> frequency by frequency:
$$
z_\omega\simeq f_0+\beta(\omega-\omega_0).
$$
Add the <group delay> at the <optical lens> to propagation through <vacuum>:
$$
\boxed{t_\omega=2\eta(\omega-\omega_0)+\frac{z_\omega}{c}
=\frac{f_0}{c}+\left(2\eta+\frac\beta c\right)(\omega-\omega_0).}
$$
Here an arbitrary common time origin has been omitted. Differentiating both equations with respect to <angular frequency> yields
$$
\boxed{\frac{v_f}{c}=\frac{\beta}{\beta+2\eta c}
=\left(1+\frac{2\eta c}{\beta}\right)^{-1}.}
$$
For $\beta>0$, define $u=2\eta c/\beta$. The velocity graph in the preceding figure has horizontal asymptote zero and a vertical asymptote at $u=-1$. For $\eta>0$, $0<v_f<c$: the higher-<frequency> components both enter later and travel farther. At $\eta=0$, $v_f=c$. For $-\beta/(2c)<\eta<0$, $v_f>c$: the farther-focusing components leave earlier, partly compensating their extra travel. At $\eta=-\beta/(2c)$ every component focuses at the same time, so the trajectory is not a finite-velocity function of time. For $\eta<-\beta/(2c)$ the farther positions focus first, giving $v_f<0$; it tends to zero from below as $\eta\to-\infty$.

A <flying focus> is a moving pattern assembled from different portions of an <optical pulse>. Each portion propagates causally at $c$ in this model. The superluminal or backward motion of the focus therefore does not describe a material object or information overtaking <light>. The calculation assumes negligible additional frequency-dependent <group delay> from the <optical lens>; any such delay must be included in $\psi$.

